L["martha"] = """As Jesus and his disciples were on their way, he came to a village where a woman named Martha opened her home to him. She had a sister called Mary. Luke doesn't say where the village was, but John tells us it was Bethany, about two miles from Jerusalem. The family were close friends of Jesus, and their brother Lazarus is mentioned elsewhere.

Hosting Jesus and his many followers was a big job. Martha went to work. Mary sat at the Lord's feet listening to what he said. In that culture, sitting at the feet of a teacher was the place for a student, and women were not normally given that place. Mary had stepped into it.

But Martha was distracted by all the preparations that had to be made. She came to him and asked, Lord, don't you care that my sister has left me to do the work by myself? Tell her to help me. Anyone who has been stuck in the kitchen while others are visiting knows how she felt. She wasn't wrong to want help, and hospitality is a good thing.

Jesus answered her gently. Martha, Martha, you are worried and upset about many things, but few things are needed, or indeed only one. Mary has chosen what is better, and it will not be taken away from her. He said her name twice. It was a tender correction, not a harsh one.

Jesus wasn't criticizing the work. He was pointing out that Martha was so consumed by serving that she had lost sight of the person she was serving. She was worried and upset, and that was the problem. He invited her to stop and sit.

Why does this matter? The story is about priorities. There is a time for serving, and there is a time for sitting still and listening. If we only do the first, we can end up resentful and empty. The one thing needed is to be with Jesus.

What happened after? We see both sisters again in John's gospel when their brother Lazarus dies. Martha is the one who goes out to meet Jesus on the road, and she makes a clear statement of faith. I believe that you are the Messiah, the Son of God, who is to come into the world. Mary falls at his feet and weeps. Jesus raises Lazarus from the dead. In a later scene, Martha is serving at a dinner again, and Mary anoints Jesus with expensive perfume. Both women are loved, and both grow.

What does this mean for us today? Most of us are more like Martha. We are busy, anxious, and feel like we are doing more than our share. Jesus doesn't scold us. He says our name, and he tells us what we need. We also don't have to pick between serving and listening. The best service comes from time spent at his feet. And this story shows us that Jesus welcomed women as students and that he wants everyone to learn from him.

That is why this story is so important. It says that being with Jesus matters more than doing things for him. And it invites us to put down what we are carrying, sit down, and listen."""
